1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,310
It's March 2021, forty-five kilometers off Western Australia,

2
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:09,070
and you're on a boat drifting above a deep underwater canyon.

3
00:00:09,720 --> 00:00:15,690
Two groups of orcas are circling. Then something enormous breaks the surface and

4
00:00:15,620 --> 00:00:20,890
exhales. A blue whale. The largest animal that has ever lived,

5
00:00:21,320 --> 00:00:25,910
an estimated sixteen meters long, nearly twice the length of the biggest orca

6
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:29,830
in the water. You'd expect a chase. It isn't one.

7
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:34,370
The orcas take turns. Some swim alongside, some underneath,

8
00:00:34,860 --> 00:00:38,990
some drop back to rest and rejoin. Over the next few hours,

9
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:42,330
they steer the whale out of the canyon toward the shallows,

10
00:00:42,580 --> 00:00:47,070
going for its jaw and its tongue. Just before three in the afternoon,

11
00:00:47,700 --> 00:00:51,490
the blue whale dies. The orcas share the meal,

12
00:00:51,420 --> 00:00:55,010
and drive off a hammerhead shark and a few pilot whales hoping for

13
00:00:54,940 --> 00:00:59,690
a bite. No walk-ins. Here's the part that doesn't add up.

14
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:03,790
In the wild, there is no confirmed case of an orca killing a

15
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:07,310
human being. So why would you never want one to notice you?

16
00:01:08,060 --> 00:01:10,030
Because the more you learn about how it hunts,

17
00:01:10,580 --> 00:01:14,230
the less that record feels like luck, and the more it feels like

18
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:20,470
a decision. First, the hardware. "Killer whale" originally meant a killer of whales,

19
00:01:20,820 --> 00:01:24,950
not people, and it's technically a dolphin, the way a tiger is technically

20
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:29,210
a cat. A big male reaches about nine and a half meters and

21
00:01:29,140 --> 00:01:32,750
can weigh more than six tonnes, roughly four family cars.

22
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:36,670
Its jaws hold forty to fifty-six curved teeth,

23
00:01:36,940 --> 00:01:40,130
each about ten centimeters long. And in short bursts,

24
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:44,090
it can pass fifty kilometers an hour. That's a city speed limit,

25
00:01:44,460 --> 00:01:49,050
underwater, with teeth. But the hardware isn't the problem.

26
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:53,430
The software is. You might assume an orca is an orca.

27
00:01:53,820 --> 00:01:58,970
It isn't. In the North Pacific alone, scientists describe populations that look almost

28
00:01:58,900 --> 00:02:03,810
identical and live completely different lives. Resident orcas eat fish,

29
00:02:04,140 --> 00:02:08,350
mostly salmon, chatter constantly, and each family keeps its own dialect.

30
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:13,190
Bigg's orcas eat marine mammals, and they do something you wouldn't expect from

31
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:19,150
the biggest hunter around: they go quiet. Researchers who tagged mammal-eating orcas in

32
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:22,110
Alaska found they stay nearly silent before a kill,

33
00:02:22,440 --> 00:02:25,330
and that they can find prey in almost total darkness,

34
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:30,290
probably by listening for it. A predator that shuts up because its dinner

35
00:02:30,220 --> 00:02:34,910
has ears. That silence is a tactic, not a limitation.

36
00:02:35,620 --> 00:02:41,010
The fish-eaters talk and echolocate constantly, because salmon don't care who's listening.

37
00:02:41,820 --> 00:02:46,430
Seals and porpoises do. One species, two playbooks,

38
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:50,570
depending on who's on the menu. And all of it is learned.

39
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:55,770
A 2015 study found that female orcas lead their groups more often than

40
00:02:55,700 --> 00:02:59,630
males, and that older females lead most when salmon get scarce,

41
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:02,250
as if experience is worth more than muscle.

42
00:03:03,060 --> 00:03:06,530
The knowledge doesn't die with the hunter. It's handed down.

43
00:03:07,220 --> 00:03:09,730
Try getting your own family to follow its elders like that.

44
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:15,530
Now watch what that knowledge turns into. Say you're a seal,

45
00:03:15,460 --> 00:03:18,890
resting on a floating slab of ice off the Antarctic Peninsula.

46
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:23,970
Researchers have watched what comes next. The orcas line up,

47
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:27,730
sometimes seven side by side, and charge the floe together.

48
00:03:28,540 --> 00:03:32,170
The wave they make either washes you into the water or breaks the

49
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:36,110
ice apart. Then they stop you climbing onto another slab,

50
00:03:36,460 --> 00:03:39,230
churning the water with their tails and blowing bubbles,

51
00:03:39,160 --> 00:03:42,790
until you're dragged down by your hind flippers and drown.

52
00:03:43,240 --> 00:03:48,230
Switch bodies. You're a sea lion pup on a beach in Argentina,

53
00:03:48,740 --> 00:03:52,850
and for a few months each year, the shoreline itself is the hunting

54
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:57,770
ground. Orcas approach swimming on their sides to hide their dorsal fins,

55
00:03:58,140 --> 00:04:00,150
ride a wave right up onto the sand,

56
00:04:00,660 --> 00:04:04,330
grab a pup, and wriggle back to deep water on the next wave.

57
00:04:05,260 --> 00:04:09,390
Experienced orcas succeed about half the time, and the young appear to learn

58
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:12,510
it from their mothers. Even the timing is planned.

59
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:15,810
They hunt in the couple of hours either side of high tide,

60
00:04:16,120 --> 00:04:18,950
when the water is deep enough close to the beach to get in

61
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:21,470
and out, and strong winds put them off,

62
00:04:21,580 --> 00:04:24,870
because bigger waves could leave them stranded too high on the sand.

63
00:04:25,620 --> 00:04:28,590
The orcas check the forecast. The pups don't.

64
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:32,790
And if you're hoping the ocean's most famous predator is at least safe,

65
00:04:33,320 --> 00:04:38,370
ask a great white. In 2009, orcas turned up at the Farallon Islands

66
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:41,550
off California, where researchers were tracking great whites.

67
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:46,470
Within eight hours, all seventeen tagged sharks were gone.

68
00:04:46,740 --> 00:04:50,630
Most didn't return until the following season, and the researchers concluded that a

69
00:04:50,560 --> 00:04:53,870
visit from orcas can keep great whites away for up to a year.

70
00:04:54,200 --> 00:04:59,470
Off South Africa, two adult males nicknamed Port and Starboard killed at least

71
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:02,930
eight great whites, eating the livers of almost all of them,

72
00:05:03,080 --> 00:05:07,290
and in some cases the hearts. Daily shark sightings at the local hotspot

73
00:05:07,220 --> 00:05:10,810
fell from more than six to about one within six months.

74
00:05:11,480 --> 00:05:15,370
The most famous predator in the sea checks for orcas before it unpacks.

75
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:20,430
Which brings us back to that canyon. In 2019 and 2021,

76
00:05:20,820 --> 00:05:25,410
researchers documented three blue whale kills by orcas off that stretch of Australia,

77
00:05:25,660 --> 00:05:29,470
reported as the first of their kind. The method was the same each

78
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:32,670
time: chase the whale until it tires, then rotate.

79
00:05:33,220 --> 00:05:35,890
Small groups take turns biting while the others rest,

80
00:05:35,820 --> 00:05:38,510
until the whale is forced under and drowns.

81
00:05:38,940 --> 00:05:42,270
Two of the three victims were young, a calf and what looked like

82
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:45,870
a year-old juvenile. The third was an apparently healthy adult.

83
00:05:46,780 --> 00:05:50,910
Wave tricks. Beach tricks. Silent stalking. Shift work.

84
00:05:51,540 --> 00:05:53,970
None of that is a reflex. It's strategy,

85
00:05:54,700 --> 00:05:58,010
and it's passed down. So where do you fit in?

86
00:05:58,460 --> 00:06:03,310
Once, as a business partner. Imagine you're a whaler in Twofold Bay,

87
00:06:03,640 --> 00:06:09,690
southeastern Australia, sometime between 1840 and 1930. A pod of orcas is helping

88
00:06:09,620 --> 00:06:13,430
you hunt baleen whales. They herd your targets toward the bay,

89
00:06:13,740 --> 00:06:18,070
sometimes swimming kilometers to alert you, and after the kill they take their

90
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:22,630
cut. Your crew calls it the Law of the Tongue: the orcas eat

91
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:25,710
the carcass's tongue and lips, and you tow the rest ashore.

92
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:29,050
A male named Old Tom is the face of the pod.

93
00:06:30,060 --> 00:06:34,330
That partnership didn't end with a fight. After a stranded whale was stabbed

94
00:06:34,260 --> 00:06:37,410
to death on a beach in 1901, the pod declined,

95
00:06:37,780 --> 00:06:40,830
and only seven of about thirty orcas returned the next year.

96
00:06:41,720 --> 00:06:45,630
By the mid-1920s, Old Tom had lost teeth gripping a tow rope.

97
00:06:46,240 --> 00:06:50,330
When he died in 1930, his body washed ashore with abscesses in his

98
00:06:50,260 --> 00:06:54,210
mouth, possibly from starvation, and the partnership ended with him.

99
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:58,030
Since then, the wild record is mostly near misses.

100
00:06:58,680 --> 00:07:04,050
In 1972, a surfer named Hans Kretschmer was bitten off California and needed

101
00:07:03,980 --> 00:07:08,830
around a hundred stitches. Experts think the orca mistook him for something else.

102
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:12,510
A hundred stitches for a case of mistaken identity.

103
00:07:13,220 --> 00:07:16,470
A free diver in New Zealand was dragged under for more than forty

104
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:18,870
seconds by an orca tangled in his line,

105
00:07:19,080 --> 00:07:21,310
which looks like an accident, not an attack.

106
00:07:22,040 --> 00:07:26,350
Those are the closest calls, and no fatal wild attack has been confirmed.

107
00:07:27,040 --> 00:07:30,250
In captivity, orcas have been involved in the deaths of trainers,

108
00:07:30,680 --> 00:07:35,470
and why is still debated. If you sail off Spain or Portugal,

109
00:07:35,860 --> 00:07:39,090
you're in the one place where orcas and people keep colliding.

110
00:07:39,740 --> 00:07:44,330
Since May 2020, a small group of Iberian orcas has been circling sailboats

111
00:07:44,260 --> 00:07:49,450
and ramming their rudders. By recent counts, that's roughly seven hundred interactions,

112
00:07:49,580 --> 00:07:52,890
and by one estimate about a fifth of them left the boat disabled.

113
00:07:53,560 --> 00:07:58,090
The whole population is under forty animals, critically endangered since 2019,

114
00:07:58,660 --> 00:08:00,950
and only about fifteen of them are doing it.

115
00:08:01,560 --> 00:08:05,870
Fifteen animals, seven hundred encounters. Impressive work ethic.

116
00:08:06,240 --> 00:08:09,210
Most of the time they leave once the rudder breaks.

117
00:08:10,100 --> 00:08:14,490
Picture being aboard: the orcas drift away, and you're the one left with

118
00:08:14,420 --> 00:08:18,910
a boat that won't steer. Reports differ on how many boats have actually

119
00:08:18,840 --> 00:08:22,830
sunk, but one went down off Lisbon in September 2025,

120
00:08:23,420 --> 00:08:26,370
and all five people aboard were rescued unharmed.

121
00:08:27,340 --> 00:08:32,350
Scientists still argue about why. The International Whaling Commission says it looks more

122
00:08:32,280 --> 00:08:37,350
like play or socializing than an attack. One leading idea is that it

123
00:08:37,280 --> 00:08:40,330
started with a single orca and spread by imitation,

124
00:08:40,800 --> 00:08:46,610
the same way wave-washing and beach-stranding spread. Another suggests the boats are practice

125
00:08:46,540 --> 00:08:51,430
equipment for young orcas learning to hunt. What nobody has found is any

126
00:08:51,360 --> 00:08:53,610
sign that it's aimed at the people on board.

127
00:08:54,180 --> 00:08:58,790
Which is reassuring, and also a slightly strange thing to find reassuring.

128
00:08:59,320 --> 00:09:03,790
So suppose an orca is heading straight for you in open water.

129
00:09:04,280 --> 00:09:08,870
Your options? Swim away? It can pass fifty kilometers an hour.

130
00:09:09,320 --> 00:09:13,950
A champion sprint swimmer manages under ten. Climb onto a floating slab of

131
00:09:13,880 --> 00:09:16,190
ice? You just saw how that goes for a seal.

132
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:19,270
Get in a boat? Ask the sailors off Gibraltar.

133
00:09:19,980 --> 00:09:25,330
Fight? Forty-odd teeth, six tonnes, and a species that has taken apart a

134
00:09:25,260 --> 00:09:28,890
blue whale. Which leaves the least dramatic option,

135
00:09:28,820 --> 00:09:32,970
and the one the evidence supports: nothing. In the wild,

136
00:09:33,300 --> 00:09:36,870
orcas have left people alone, and nobody can fully explain why.

137
00:09:37,440 --> 00:09:41,950
Keep your distance, follow local wildlife rules, and let it decide you aren't

138
00:09:41,880 --> 00:09:45,810
interesting. Be boring. You've been training for this your whole life.

139
00:09:46,820 --> 00:09:49,790
To be fair to the orcas, they have more reason to fear you

140
00:09:49,720 --> 00:09:53,610
than you have to fear them. A population of fewer than forty animals

141
00:09:53,540 --> 00:09:57,850
can't afford to pick fights with sailboats. And what orcas do out there

142
00:09:57,780 --> 00:10:02,990
shapes everything around them. When two orcas cleared great whites out of one

143
00:10:02,920 --> 00:10:07,810
South African bay, smaller bronze whaler sharks moved into the role the great

144
00:10:07,740 --> 00:10:11,390
whites had held. Add or remove one top predator,

145
00:10:11,320 --> 00:10:14,870
and the whole food web rearranges itself around it.

146
00:10:15,280 --> 00:10:18,530
So no, you don't want an orca to notice you.

147
00:10:18,520 --> 00:10:22,190
But the ocean you're swimming in works better with them in it.

148
00:10:23,080 --> 00:10:28,530
Go back to that canyon, March 2021. A blue whale surfaces and the

149
00:10:28,460 --> 00:10:34,510
orcas arrive. What's unsettling isn't the violence. It's the teamwork: patience,

150
00:10:34,900 --> 00:10:38,590
rotation, teaching, a shared meal at the end.

151
00:10:39,340 --> 00:10:41,870
That's what's looking at you when an orca looks at you.

152
00:10:42,240 --> 00:10:45,710
Not a monster. Something that plans, and has decided,

153
00:10:45,980 --> 00:10:48,690
so far, that you aren't worth the effort.

154
00:10:49,762 --> 00:10:55,997
Let's hope it keeps that opinion. If there's another predator you want us

155
00:10:55,888 --> 00:11:00,059
to investigate, tell me in the comments. And if you want to keep

156
00:10:59,950 --> 00:11:03,653
going deeper, the next video is right here.
